The code used to produce the result can vary depending on the implementation that serves the specific purpose of extracting and processing the necessary information from the comment and constructing the appropriate Debug.Assert statement. For a simplistic solution, the code below can be used:

C# Code:
```cs
static void AddAssertsToComments(string code)
{
    var lines = code.Split(new string[] {"\r\n", "\n"}, StringSplitOptions.None).ToList();
    for (int i = 0; i < lines.Count; i++)
    {
        string line = lines[i];
        if(line.TrimStart().StartsWith("// @@@"))
        {
            string comment = line.Trim().Remove(0, "// @@@".Length);
            comment = comment.Replace("x", "New_Ret").Replace("y", "Old_arrListCount").Replace("sum", "New_arrListCount"); 
            var assertion = string.Format("Debug.Assert({0});", comment);
            lines[i] = assertion;
        }
    }
    Console.WriteLine(string.Join(Environment.NewLine, lines));
}
```

This function reads the code, identifies lines with comments that start "// @@@", extracts the condition from the comment, replaces instances of "x", "y", and "sum" with the corresponding variables in the code, and inserts a Debug.Assert statement where the comment was located in the original code.

Before using this approach, consider if the code requires additional modifications to use Debug.Assert correctly and make sure that the variables being used are existing.--------------------
